WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.220
JavaScript output needs special care because the destination is executable code.

2
00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:11.080
Untrusted data placed into a script context can become code if it is not represented correctly.

3
00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:19.020
Quotation marks, backslashes, line breaks, template delimiters, and closing script sequences can all affect parsing.

4
00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:22.880
The safer pattern is to avoid concatenating data into scripts.

5
00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:32.100
Keep data separate from code and use framework-supported data binding, DOM APIs that treat values as text, or safe serialization patterns.

6
00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:35.340
JSON should be produced with a proper serializer.

7
00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:42.320
Serializers understand quoting, escaping, data types, arrays, objects, and null values.

8
00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:56.180
Hand-building JSON strings is brittle because one unexpected quote, backslash, or control character can break structure or create an injection path when the JSON is embedded into another context.

9
00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:05.800
Even serialized JSON needs attention when placed inside an HTML page or script block because the surrounding context may add additional requirements.

10
00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:11.040
Data-in-script patterns should be minimized and reviewed carefully.

11
00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:19.460
If a server renders initial state into a page, make sure the framework provides a safe mechanism or the output is encoded for the exact script context.

12
00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:24.040
Avoid inline script injection by separating static code from dynamic data.

13
00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:35.260
Prefer external scripts with data passed through safe channels, such as JSON endpoints, data attributes handled by the framework, or text-only DOM APIs.

14
00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:40.800
The design goal is simple: data should stay data, and code should stay code.
